    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_113724e6136c47938ab7f50e454ed17f1e58ba29d7dac5242c4bf3d132fc1083",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The two constructions support a precise analogy, not an identification: a vanishing exponent on Möbius coefficients preserves fixed-point or fixed-coordinate targets while making reciprocal-zeta tails estimable, but only a square-integrable majorant upgrades those local limits to the norm required by an RH criterion. Báez-Duarte supplies that majorant after Mellin-Plancherel under RH; Habsieger leaves the corresponding intermediate-coordinate dominator as an explicit criterion assumption.",
