    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_1643ba318885c7118242411194737bfd252731806964bd410dad8c49c53319d9",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The relation is a concrete analogy between distinct extremal-product programs: small-prime products concentrate multiplicative observables near an RH-sensitive boundary, so a one-sided criterion needs the sign and rate of approach, not only its limiting scale. Lagarias describes how smooth colossally abundant products reflect prime-counting fluctuations; the Nicolas-based preprint demonstrates the logical failure directly, since R(M_x) tending to e^gamma/zeta(2) gives no guarantee that its possible deficit is smaller than the explicit Euler-tail surplus.",
