    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_4f923474a80cc3868ff42660d964870691786c56b0103cdafcbc2e6bf44083a2",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The sources support a concrete contrast between proof-closing and exploratory finite computation. In Lagarias's criterion, analytic inequalities cover every n>=5041, so the reported check of 1<=n<=5040 exhausts the residual obligation. For c_k, the source gives no analytic cutoff: a hypothetical high off-line zero may have a strongly suppressed coefficient and only dominate at an unbounded later index, so a long finite run cannot close the universal tail.",
